  <string-array name="preguntas">
    <item>Can it connect to my bank?</item>
    <item>Where is my data?</item>
    <item>Which method should I choose?</item>
    <item>How do I log my credit card?</item>
    <item>Why doesn’t the minimum payment match my statement?</item>
    <item>What is the 4×1000?</item>
    <item>What’s the difference between saving and a transfer?</item>
  </string-array>
  <string-array name="respuestas">
    <item>No. In Colombia, open finance only covers regulated companies, and a free app with no server can’t connect. This way nobody has your passwords, either. Logging entries is quick with the app’s keypad.</item>
    <item>Only on your phone. The app has no internet permission. Save backups from Profile → Backup.</item>
    <item>For control, Envelopes. For something simple, the 50/30/20 rule. If you like writing and thinking about the month, kakeibo. If you only want to see where it goes, Just track. You can change it any time.</item>
    <item>Card purchases are expenses from the card account (with their instalments). Paying the card is a transfer from your bank to the card: it isn’t another expense, you already counted it when you bought.</item>
    <item>Because every bank calculates it its own way. The app estimates it with the percent you set on the account; the real figure is on your statement.</item>
    <item>Colombia’s financial transactions tax: 4 pesos per 1,000 leaving a bank account. Turn it on in Profile and the app logs it for you. Many savings accounts have an exemption.</item>
    <item>Saving sets money aside for a goal: it leaves this month’s available money and adds to the goal. A transfer just moves money between your accounts, and nothing else changes.</item>
  </string-array>
